{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "notes": "Every surface whose native port is under the visual gate (#874, ADR-0118). A surface appears here before it is switched on, and docs/mobile/screens.json may only set \"enabled\": true when every state this file declares for that surface carries its evidence. Each entry names the web route, the contract (shared/visual/<surface>.json - the states, and for each state the elements and properties both clients must agree on), the test that draws the app side, and, per state, its tolerances: tolerance.length is the CSS-pixel drift allowed on a box, and tolerance.pixels is that state's recorded raster baseline - the share of the capture that differs at the best alignment, measured from the pair that passed, with the date it was recorded. Evidence lives in e2e/__visual__/<surface>/<state>/ and is recorded by `npm run visual:capture` (page) and the app's own visual test (screen). `frame` is the chrome every primary screen wears (#902): not a surface, because nothing routes to it and perf/surfaces.json gives each route one owner, so it is recorded once on `/` and gated by its contract - with \"raster\": \"none\" and a `why` when the page pixels are the page content rather than the chrome. Every contract in shared/visual (a surface, or the frame) also carries a motion section beside its states (#903): motion.notes says what the surface's motion is, or that it has none, and motion.entries is a list of { element?, gesture, moves, same } - element names the box the movement belongs to when it has one, gesture is what starts the movement, moves is what changes, and same is what \"the same\" means for the two clients. The notes carry the surface-level decision; the backfill (#970) adds the per-movement entries. check:visual requires the section and checks its shape; it cannot measure motion, so each entry is compared by hand against the page.",
